<h2 data-start="1949" data-end="1985" class=""><strong data-start="1952" data-end="1985">III. Objections and Responses</strong></h2>
<h3 data-start="1986" data-end="2055" class="">1. <strong data-start="1993" data-end="2053">Substrate Neutrality Objection (from classical comp-sci)</strong></h3>
<ul data-start="2056" data-end="2143">
<li data-start="2056" data-end="2143" class="">
<p data-start="2058" data-end="2143" class=""><em data-start="2058" data-end="2065">Claim</em>: Computation is substrate-independent; why focus on physical reappropriation?</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="2145" data-end="2382" class=""><strong data-start="2145" data-end="2157">Response</strong>:<br data-start="2158" data-end="2161">
Substrate neutrality <strong data-start="2182" data-end="2194">presumes</strong> fixed interpretive layers. Polycompilation <strong data-start="2238" data-end="2265">describes the emergence</strong> of those layers. Itâ€™s a theory of <em data-start="2300" data-end="2305">how</em> new levels of interpretation ariseâ€”not just <em data-start="2350" data-end="2368">how they operate</em> once defined.</p>
