Summary
This briefing document outlines the concept of ontological polycompilation, a new framework that extends Constructor Theory to explain how knowledge and complex behaviors emerge. The central idea is that this happens through the recursive reappropriation of physical substrates into symbolic representations, a process driven by polyconstructors and the reappropriation operator (R). The paper utilizes a category-theoretic framework to formalize these ideas and connects them to Deutsch's work on knowledge creation and its inherent risks, suggesting implications for understanding embodied cognition.

